<table><tr><td align="left">
    

<CENTER><STRONG>Training Thunderstorms and the Heavy Rain Event of May 14-16, 2001</STRONG></CENTER>
<P>
<CENTER><FONT SIZE="-1"><EM>Jeff Andresen, Steve Aichele, and Aaron Pollyea, Geography</EM></FONT></CENTER>
<P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Between Monday afternoon the 14<SUP>th</SUP> and Wednesday afternoon the 16<SUP>th</SUP>, heavy rain fell over large areas of Lower
Michigan.  Some of the 24- and 48-hour totals were record-breaking, with 2-day totals in excess of 6 inches at a few
locations.  The heavy rain caused extensive flooding and crop damage in some areas.  Meteorologically, the event
was the result of a series of training thunderstorms that formed along and just east of a warm front which Monday
afternoon stretched from the western Upper Peninsula southeastward through the southwestern corner of Lower
Michigan.  So-called 'training thunderstorms' are named after their appearance on radar in which individual storms
develop and move across the same area. This repeated movement over the same area often results in torrential
rainfalls and flash flooding.  In the Midwest region, this mechanism is responsible for the majority of record heavy
rainfall events. </FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1"><center><img src="./fig05-01.gif"></center></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Figure 1.  Radar reflectivities and relative echo directional movement at 4:25 PM May 15<SUP>th</SUP>.  Brightest echoes (red
and orange areas in color) correspond to heaviest precipitation rates (Figure courtesy of Intellicast, Inc.).</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Monday afternoon the 14th, surface air temperatures varied as much as 30F across the Lower Peninsula, ranging
from the upper 80's in extreme southwestern corner to the upper 50's only 200 miles away to the northeast. 
Dewpoint temperatures, a measure of water vapor (the 'raw material' for precipitation) rose to the low to mid 60's F
in southwestern Lower Michigan in association with strong southerly winds and the northward  transport of large
amounts of Gulf of Mexico-origin moisture.  With strong low-level (less than 5,000 above sea level) convergence of
winds along the front and the resulting lifting, thunderstorms formed in a northwest to southwest line along the area
of greatest temperature discontinuity.  There were three major episodes of thunderstorms; the first late Monday
evening into Tuesday morning, Tuesday afternoon and evening, and finally early Wednesday morning.  The unique
banding character of thunderstorm formation is evident in the radar depiction of the second thunderstorm episode
Tuesday afternoon in Figure 1.  Because of northwesterly middle and upper level winds, the movement of the
individual thunderstorms was towards the southeast almost directly along the line of convective formation, which
resulted in the training pattern.  </FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Estimated accumulated precipitation totals during the 48 hour period from Monday evening through Wednesday
evening are given in Figure 2.  These estimates are based on 2.5-mile spatial resolution National Weather Service
NEXRAD Stage III radar reflectivities.  While not actual precipitation measurements, the estimates are calibrated
with available rain gage data and quality-controlled for known contamination problems such as hail (which did
occur in the Monday evening/Tuesday morning event) and should at least be 'in the ballpark' with actual totals. 
Overall, the estimates still offer an analysis at a spatial resolution not obtainable with conventional rain gage data. </FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">In Figure 2, the spatial patterns of precipitation resulting from the training thunderstorm episodes are clearly
evident. The colored/shaded areas depict all areas with greater than 1 inch of total precipitation. Chronologically,
the first thunderstorm episode brought heavy rain along a line roughly from Oceana County southeastward to
Hillsdale County.  The second episode (illustrated in Figure 1) occurred along a narrow line from Osceola County
southeastward to Monroe County.  Early Wednesday morning, a third episode caused heavy rainfall totals in a more
widespread area from the central Upper Peninsula southeastward through northwestern Lower Michigan to the
Indiana/Ohio/Michigan border area. Overall, large sections of northwestern, west central, and south central Lower
Michigan received more than 3 inches.  The heaviest rainfall total in this period occurred across southern sections of
Oceana County and along the Gratiot/Isabella County line, where more than 9 inches were estiamted.  In stark
contrast, rainfall totals across sections of extreme southwestern and eastern Lower Michigan were in many cases
less than 0.25 inches.  Climatologically, greatest 10-year 48-hour precipitation totals (an event that would be
expected to occur only once every 10 years on the average) range from about 4.0 inches in southwestern Lower
Michigan to less than 3.5 inches in northeastern sections of the state.  The 100-year 48-hour event event ranges from
7.0 inches in southwestern sections of the state to less than 5.0 inches in the northeast.  Thus, at least in sections of
west central and central Lower Michigan, this event should be considered very rare by historical standards.</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Finally, in the 'for what it's worth' category, we offer another perspective of this precipitation event. One of the
magic tricks included in a Geographic Information System software package such as that used to create Figure 2 is
the ability to spatially sum up all of the precipitation that fell in this period, which in this case is the total volume of
rain water that fell within the political boundaries of the state of Michigan during the given 48-hour period. Not
including what fell over any of the surrounding Great Lakes, this number turns out to be <EM>264 billion cubic feet</EM> of
water. If spread out evenly across all of the land area in the state of Michigan, this would amount to a rainfall event
of just under 2 inches statewide.  Lastly, given all the current national publicity concerning the lack of energy, let's
also remember that 64,000 BTUs of energy were used to evaporate each cubic foot of water (mostly over the Gulf
of Mexico and land between Michigan and the Gulf Region) and released once again when the water vapor
condensed into clouds and precipitation.  This energy was originally provided directly and indirectly from the sun. 
For this volume of precipitation, and given a total nationwide energy consumption for the entire year in 1999of 9.7 x
10<SUP>16</SUP> BTUs (E.P.A., 2000), that translates to an estimated energy release (during the series of precipitation events)
equivalent to 64 days of total nationwide energy consumption.  </FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1"></FONT><FONT SIZE="-1"><center><img src="./fig05-02.jpg"></center></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Figure 2.  Total estimated precipitation (inches) from 08:00 PM Monday May 14<SUP>th</SUP> through 08:00PM Wednesday
May 16<SUP>th</SUP>.  Raw data courtesy of the National Weather Service North Central Regional River Forecast Center,
Chanhassen, MN.</FONT></P>


